## STEP 6 — LEARNING

- [LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/auth/v1/*: reflected-origin credentialed CORS is real and **browser-exploitable**, because `apikey` is accepted as a query parameter — documented by the gateway's own 401 hint — which downgrades the request to a CORS-simple one and sidesteps the `ACAO: *`-without-`ACAC` preflight. My prior belief that "the credential channel is absent, so this is bounded" was wrong.
- [LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ own KB (self-correction, two directions): the "wildcard without ACAC" record is falsified by direct probe (reflection + `ACAC: true`, arbitrary and `null`), and the "gateway-wide" framing is falsified because storage and REST require the `Authorization` header and serve `ACAO: *`. The defect is GoTrue-plane-specific.
- [LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.storage.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3: the RLS-bypassing SigV4 plane is deployed, pre-auth reachable, and **closed to the publishable key** — `?list-type=2` with apikey + Bearer → `403 Missing signature`. Hypothesis dropped 60→40 because the public key does not reach it and key existence remains unproven.
- [LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/auth/v1/settings: `saml_private_key_next_configured: true` alongside `saml_enabled: false`; first read of this field. A private key is provisioned for a disabled plane.
- [LEARN] REJECTED OATH @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co: SSO/ACS/SSO-metadata all return `404 saml_provider_disabled`, so the configured SAML key is unreachable and IdP-initiated assertion injection is closed on measurement.
- [LEARN] REJECTED AUTH @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co: `phone: false` and `phone_autoconfirm: false` falsify the SMS-OTP autoconfirm ATO class; GoTrue route map completed with `/auth/v1/reverify` absent on gw v2, `/auth/v1/user/identities` 401, `/auth/v1/otp` POST-only and deliberately not sent.
- [LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ kurs.onecode.de / cto.onecode.de: no deploy, day-14. Main chunk `f916f314ea61a8c5…` (154 581 B) and sink `5a72d2cd8738ecad…` (13 880 B) both byte-identical; `/login` 200 no `Set-Cookie`; cto CNAME day-49 with 409. Build-diffing stays event-triggered.
- [LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.storage.supabase.co: the `<Resource>` element in S3 error XML is a reflection of the client-supplied path, not a bucket-existence signal — recorded so a future cycle does not misread it as an oracle.

## STEP 7 — RISK

[RISK] onecode: 46 — down two, and deliberately. Demonstrated impact is unchanged: no user data read, no course material exposed, no ATO, no key compromise. What changed is accuracy in both directions. I raised the top finding by showing the credential channel the program believed absent does exist in a usable form — `?apikey=` is accepted, so the GoTrue CORS defect is browser-exploitable from any origin including `null` with only a publicly-shipped key. I simultaneously cut its scope: the defect is GoTrue-only, storage and REST still require a header and serve `ACAO: *`, and the exposed data today is auth configuration, not user records. The escalation to user data is provably blocked because a real bearer must travel in a header, reintroducing the preflight barrier the query form cannot bypass. Separately I closed three latent auth classes on live data (SAML assertion injection, SMS autoconfirm, public-key access to the S3 plane), which is a real reduction in the program's uncertainty even though none of them was ever live. The honest read is that the owner has one config defect worth fixing, one first-party forced-login flaw needing two test accounts to prove, and no demonstrated path to customer data.
