**STEP 4 — SELF-CRITIQUE**

- `[PARKED]` "CORS cache-poisoning amplification": re-tested on **both** planes, including the REST plane the earlier falsification never covered. `cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC` everywhere. Genuinely dead, and this cycle found a second reason to believe it: the REST plane's missing `vary: Origin` would only matter to a shared cache that does not exist on this path. Latent hardening defect, not exploitable.
- `[PARKED]` "REST plane `vary: Origin` omission": real header defect, but `DYNAMIC` caching means no amplifier. Folded into hypothesis 1 as remediation detail rather than a standalone finding — reporting it alone would be descriptive headers only, which is out of scope.
- `[PARKED]` "anon signed-URL minting via `POST /storage/v1/object/sign`": still awaiting explicit approval, third cycle. It is a POST and I will not run one under passive-only rules. The GET side is already proven to reach token validation and to reject a forged signature, so the class is bounded either way.
- `[PARKED]` RLS cross-tenant BOLA: untouched and deliberately not re-scored. Every cheap route to a second principal is closed by live test — `external.anonymous_users: false`, `disable_signup: true`, GoTrue admin plane 401, HS256 secret guess 403/401 across 4 candidates, PostgREST schema 401 above the table layer, REST monitor closed after 26 probes never returning 200+rows. AUTH_HELPED only.
- `[PARKED]` `cto.onecode.de` dangling CNAME (day-48): HUMAN_ONLY. Confidence 58 held flat, not inflated by 48 cycles of byte-identical output.
- `[PARKED]` Supabase public-bucket exposure: oracle exists on 3 routes, 46 names excluded, bucket name unrecoverable from pre-auth bundles. Closed.
- `[FINAL]` Survivors ranked: (1) reflected credentialed CORS across GoTrue + REST, conf 74, PASSIVE, header-auth leg newly unblocked, one config fix. (2) S3 SigV4 plane, conf 40, HUMAN_ONLY. (3) Fragment session injection, conf 55, AUTH_HELPED. Hypothesis 3 is ranked below 2 on testability, not impact.

**STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION**

- `[NEXT]` HUMAN: request two invited test accounts from the program owner (`bugs.olivermaicher.eu`). This is the single unblock that resolves three open classes at once — the RLS cross-tenant BOLA (conf 65, needs two distinct `authenticated` principals), the fragment session injection (needs a real token pair to demonstrate forced-login), and the header-auth CORS read (needs a real bearer to close the escalation leg). Every one of those is blocked on the same input, and no amount of further passive probing substitutes for it.

**STEP 6 — LEARNING**

- `[LEARN]` REJECTED MISCONFIG @ `aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co` — "GoTrue serves wildcard `ACAO: *` without `ACAC: true`" is **falsified**. Live simple requests reflect arbitrary Origin with `ACAC: true`; the wildcard form belongs to `OPTIONS` preflight responses only. The KB had two agents measuring different request types and recording both as simple-request findings.
- `[LEARN]` ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/rest/v1/*` — the REST plane reflects arbitrary Origin, contradicting the catalogued "wildcard-without-credentials" characterisation. On table paths it also omits `vary: Origin` while reflecting it. `cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC` keeps this non-exploitable today, so it is a hardening defect, not an amplifier.
- `[LEARN]` ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/auth/v1/user` — CORS headers are emitted on the 403 rejection state, so the reflection is applied before authentication rather than only on the anon path. Combined with the preflight granting `authorization` under `ACAO: *`, the header-auth leg of the CORS finding is readable cross-origin; my prior cycle's contrary conclusion is retracted as an incorrect reading of CORS credential semantics.
- `[LEARN]` REJECTED MISCONFIG @ `kurs.onecode.de` / `cto.onecode.de` — no deploy, no delta. Main chunk `f916f314ea61a8c5…` and sink chunk `5a72d2cd8738ecad…` both byte-identical at day-14 (no deploy since 2026-09-19 11:33Z); cto CNAME → `cname.perspective-dns.com` day-48 with HTTP 409. Build-diffing remains event-triggered, not time-triggered.

**STEP 7 — RISK**

`[RISK]` onecode: **47** — up one, narrowly, and for a reason I want to be precise about rather than inflate. Demonstrated impact is still unchanged: no user data read, no course material exposed, no ATO, no credential compromise, and the credential channel on the cookie-bearing host remains clean (kurs.onecode.de emits no `access-control-*` on a foreign Origin, confirmed this cycle). What changed is that a finding the program had twice recorded with opposite conclusions, and which one agent had closed on an incorrect reading of CORS, is now measured cleanly: reflection plus `ACAC: true` on both the anon and header-auth paths, on two planes, including `null` origin. So the config defect is real and wider than catalogued, and the header-auth leg is no longer provably blocked. I am holding the score near flat because the escalation still requires a bearer token I have never had, and the cache amplifier remains dead. The single most useful thing the owner can do is set an origin allowlist and hand over two test accounts — one is a one-line config change, the other converts three parked hypotheses into resolved ones.
