           the same URL each got their own `ACAO`, so no poisoning is currently observable, and every response
           measured is `cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC` with a 503/401 status, none of which is
           cacheable-by-default under RFC 9111. So this is a latent amplifier, not a demonstrated cache
           poisoning: the `vary` omission means any cache that ever stores a REST response could serve one
           origin's `ACAO` to another, and the absence of `vary: Origin` also defeats browser per-origin
           normalization. It is reported separately from the allowlist finding because the fix differs —
           `vary: Origin` must be set on the reflection, and an allowlist would not by itself guarantee it.
evidence_needed: A cached reflection. Concretely: any PostgREST response that returns 200 or 404 (both
           cacheable-by-default) while reflecting `ACAO`, served to a second origin. Today the schema cache is
           down (503 PGRST002) and the root path returns 401, so no such response exists to observe — the
           defect is provable from headers alone and exploitable only once the schema cache recovers or on any
           table that starts answering.
verify_steps:
  1) DONE: `curl -D- -H "apikey: <pub>" -H "Origin: https://evil.example" <SUP>/rest/v1/profiles` → `ACAO: https://evil.example`, `vary: Accept-Encoding` (no `Origin`), `cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC`.
  2) DONE: same URL with `Origin: https://second.example` → `ACAO: https://second.example`. No cross-serving observed.
  3) DONE: `HEAD` form → same missing `vary: Origin`.
  4) DONE: `/rest/v1/rpc/nope` and `/rest/v1/nonexistent_table_zz1` → 503, same missing `vary: Origin` — 404 (cacheable by default) never reached.
  5) DONE: contrast `GET <SUP>/auth/v1/settings` same host → `vary: Origin, Accept-Encoding`. The defect is REST-plane-specific.
  6) NOT POSSIBLE TODAY: no 200/404 REST response exists to cache, so poisoning cannot be demonstrated.
impact: LOW now, MEDIUM latent. No cacheable response currently exists, so there is no live amplifier; a
       shared cache or a future CDN policy change would turn a routine cache hit into cross-origin `ACAO`
       confusion on the plane that serves table data. Cheap to fix while the code is being touched for the
       allowlist. Not reportable on its own today — reported as hardening guidance attached to the allowlist
       finding, and flagged as a re-test trigger the moment the PostgREST schema cache recovers.
testability: PASSIVE

