## STEP 6 — LEARNING

```
[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/auth/v1/* + /rest/v1/*: the reflected-origin
        credentialed CORS finding now has a proven credential channel on BOTH planes. `?apikey=<publishable>`
        is accepted as a query parameter (401 hint names it), which removes the preflight requirement entirely
        and leaves a *simple* cross-origin request with a readable response. Measured with a control: no
        `Origin` header yields zero `access-control-*` headers, so reflection is Origin-driven. CORS headers
        are emitted before the JWT check (403 `bad_jwt` carries ACAO+ACAC). `null` origin reflected on both
        planes, so the primitive needs no attacker domain. Storage excluded (400 requires the header).
        kurs.onecode.de excluded (0 access-control headers; OPTIONS 400). Still no user data read.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/rest/v1/*: the PostgREST plane reflects Origin
        while omitting `vary: Origin` (`vary: Accept-Encoding` only), on 5 paths and the HEAD form, while
        GoTrue on the same host emits `vary: Origin, Accept-Encoding` correctly. Latent cache-key defect; not
        exploitable today because every response is 503/401 DYNAMIC and none is cacheable-by-default.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/storage/v1/*: "the query-string apikey channel
        extends to storage" is false. `GET /storage/v1/bucket?apikey=<publishable>` → 400
        `{"code":"InvalidRequest","message":"headers must have required property 'authorization'"}`. Storage
        requires the `authorization` header, so the no-preflight channel is GoTrue+PostgREST only. The prior
        cycle's scoping (storage requires a header) is re-confirmed, not widened.

[LEARN] REJECTED OATH @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co: GoTrue `/auth/v1/authorize` re-confirmed inert on
        live data — `?provider=github&redirect_to=https://evil.example/` → 400 `validation_failed "Unsupported
        provider: provider is not enabled"`, no `Location` header, `ACAO: null` reflected. No redirect_uri
        validation, no state, no redirect primitive. 46-day-old conclusion holds.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ kurs.onecode.de: no deploy. Main chunk `f916f314ea61a8c5…` and sink chunk
        `5a72d2cd8738ecad…` both byte-identical, day-15. `HEAD /login` 200 with no `Set-Cookie`; pre-auth set
        unchanged at {/login,/passwort-vergessen,/datenschutz,/rechtliches}. Build-diffing stays
        event-triggered, not time-triggered — the time budget belongs on the two-account unblock.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ own KB (self-audit, corrects an earlier entry): the KB line "CORS cache-poisoning
        — `vary: Origin` + `cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC` confirmed; no cache amplification" was measured on
        GoTrue only and is over-broad. On the PostgREST plane `vary: Origin` is absent while `ACAO` is
        reflected. The no-amplification conclusion holds today (DYNAMIC, non-cacheable statuses) but not for
        the reason the KB states.
```

## STEP 7 — RISK

```
[RISK] onecode: 47 — up one, for scope, not for access. Demonstrated impact is unchanged: no user data read,
       no course material exposed, no ATO, no key compromise, no takeover. What changed is the reach of the
       single configuration defect. I widened the cross-origin channel from the identity plane to the
       table-data plane — `?apikey=` is accepted on both, so a simple request from any origin, including
       `null`, reads PostgREST responses without a preflight, and today it reaches the PGRST002 schema-cache
       error rather than rows only because the cache is down. I also found a second, independent defect on that
       plane: it reflects Origin while omitting `vary: Origin`, which GoTrue on the same host gets right. The
       escalation path is unchanged in kind but wider in degree: any bearer token that leaks by an ordinary
       means becomes usable from any website to read `auth.users` and every table. I am deliberately not going
       higher — the honest read is that the program has one fixable config defect on a public-data plane, one
       first-party forced-login flaw needing two test accounts to prove, and a third hypothesis whose entire
       premise (that S3 credentials exist) is unknown to both me and the program. The one input that would
       convert any of this from hypothesis to impact is still two invited accounts from the owner.
```
