- 2026-10-03 REJECTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/auth/v1/oauth: my own previous-cycle claim that OAuth reflection is **exact-route, not prefix-mounted** is **falsified by my own queued probe**. `oauth/token/extra`, `oauth/authorize/extra` and `oauth/userinfo/extra` all return Kong-local `feature_disabled` with reflected `ACAO` + `ACAC: true`. I generalised from `oauth/zzz_ctrl_9x7` — an *unregistered name*, therefore a fallthrough case — and mistook "unregistered names fall through" for "prefixes are not mounted". The correct rule is that every one of the 6 pre-gate names **is** prefix-mounted, so the reflecting path space is unbounded at depth rather than a closed 6-route list. Cost of the error: one cycle of an over-narrow scope claim that a bounty triager would have had to correct before reporting.
- 2026-10-03 ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known: the pre-gate reflecting surface is **unbounded and prefix-mounted**, verified to depth 5 (`a/b/c/d/e`), and the RFC 8414 host segment is **not load-bearing** — `oauth-authorization-server/wrong-host.example` behaves identically to the real hostname. Every unbounded path returns a Kong-local `404 page not found` with no body data, so this enlarges the *CORS* surface without enlarging the *data* surface. The forward-looking consequence is the real finding: a route added later under any of these 6 prefixes inherits the permissive credentialed policy automatically, which is a durable class rather than 6 fixed endpoints.
- 2026-10-03 ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ aygnpacdkgtsfnhgcyjc.supabase.co/auth/v1: **the mechanism is gate ordering, not CORS tiers** — my three-tier framing was descriptive, not causal. `401 {"message":"No API key found in request"}` + `ACAO: *` + no `ACAC` is GoTrue's global apikey gate running *before* routing; Kong's OAuth/OpenID routes are matched pre-gate, which is the sole reason they reflect credentials without a key. Decisive evidence: GoTrue's own `/auth/v1/token` and `/auth/v1/register` are in the *safe* tier on both exact and subpath matches, while the identical subpath shape under a Kong route name returns `ACAC: true`. Reframing this way changes the recommended fix from "add an allowlist to these paths" to "enforce the allowlist after route resolution", which is the difference between a fix that works and one that looks comprehensive.
